// A curated catalog of CDP effects exposed to consuming apps and tests.
//
// Each effect names a CDP program, an argument template, and its parameters.
// `domain: 'spectral'` effects are wrapped in a pvoc analysis/resynthesis chain;
// `mono: true` marks programs that only accept mono input, so multichannel
// sources are processed per channel and recombined (see applyEffect).
//
// Arg template tokens: '$IN'/'$OUT' (virtual file paths), '$IN2' (second source
// for `inputs: 2` effects), '$INS' (space-joined list of the variadic inputs, for
// `variadicInputs` effects), '$DATA' (a text data file generated by `data(values)`),
// a literal string, { p: 'name' } which is replaced by the named parameter's
// current value, or { p: 'name', flag: '-s' } which becomes the value attached to
// a flag, e.g. '-s0.5' (the form CDP uses for optional switches).
//
// Most effects take one WAV and return one WAV (preserving channel count). A few
// need extra machinery, marked by optional entry fields:
//   `multiOut: true`  — the program writes a generic outfile name → several
//                        numbered files; applyEffect returns
//                        `{ outputs: Uint8Array[], names: string[] }` instead of a
//                        single Uint8Array. '$OUT' maps to the generic stem '/out'.
//   `variadicInputs`  — extra.inputs is an array of WAVs staged as /in1.wav… and
//                        referenced by '$INS'.
//   `mixChain`        — a { build, render } pair that stages N inputs, builds a
//                        CDP mixfile, then renders it to a multichannel WAV.
//   `setsChannels`/`outChans` — the effect defines its own output width (the
//                        catalog test then asserts that width, not the source's).

import { decodeAudio, encodeWav } from './wav.js';

/** @typedef {{name:string,label:string,min:number,max:number,default:number,step?:number,help?:string,hardMin?:number,hardMax?:number}} Param */
// `help` is a short, plain-language description of what the parameter does (shown
// in a consuming app's curated ? panel). Keep it one or two sentences, no CDP jargon.
//
// `min`/`max` are a *curated* span — the musically useful part of what the
// program accepts, sized so a slider's travel is worth having. They are usually
// narrower than CDP's own limits, and deliberately so: audit-param-ranges.mjs
// treats a range wider than the engine's as an error and a narrower one as an
// advisory. `hardMin`/`hardMax` optionally record the engine's real limit (from
// the program's own `ap->lo[]`/`ap->hi[]` table, or its manpage) so a consumer
// can offer to unlock the full span; where they are absent, an unlocking UI just
// stops clamping and lets CDP reject what it will not take.

